Because /etc/server forks and execs the parent for a new connecion and keeps the child, you end up with a telnetd session having a parent of a previous telnet/ftp/xx session.This means when a telnet child shell dies and sends SIGDEAD, the parent needs to determine if this is from its child OR another session has died... See code for SIGDEAD handling for more info..telnet session does not always flush all output..select() fails to timeout sometimes..it appears to be after lots of pty output but not sure.  Any input (keypress) makesselect() return with a ready FD and output continues again.Launching nfsd from commandline works fine. Launching from /etc/server where server passes in initial socket handle seems to work less well.